{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"34cfa167\" lines=\"94-117\">\n<description>\nComplete a four-corner framed rectangle from one decorated anchor rectangle and its diagonal mate.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Two same-color solid rectangular anchors of the same size sit on opposite\n  diagonal corners, with the first anchor above-left of the second.\n- The upper-left anchor has two one-cell-thick vertical control bars to its\n  right and two one-cell-thick horizontal control bars below it.\n\nRule:\n- Copy the anchor rectangle into the two missing corners of the implied\n  larger rectangle.\n- Repeat the right-side control bars horizontally between the left and right\n  anchors, using their spacing as the period, and draw the outer top and\n  bottom borders in the first right-control color.\n- Repeat the below-side control bars vertically between the top and bottom\n  anchors, using the same period, and draw the outer left and right borders\n  in the first below-control color.\n\nKey insight: The small control bars are the first two ticks of two periodic\nframe patterns; the diagonal anchor supplies the opposite corner and the\nmissing two anchor copies.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"95755ff2\" lines=\"45-60\">\n<description>\nPaint straight rays from colored side markers into the hollow diamond frame until an interior foreground pixel blocks each ray.\n\nInput structure\n- The background contains one complete one-pixel-thick diamond frame.\n- Colored marker pixels outside the diamond sit on the top, bottom, left, or right side of the frame's bounding box.\n- Any non-background, non-frame pixels inside the diamond are blockers that remain unchanged.\n\nRule\n- Each outside marker emits inward perpendicular to its side of the diamond bounding box.\n- Along that row or column, paint only background pixels strictly inside the diamond with the marker color.\n- Stop a ray when it reaches an existing interior foreground pixel; keep the frame and all original colored pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The diamond frame defines a strict interior, while the side markers are column or row emitters whose color fills the visible interior interval until blocked.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b5033c1\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nStraighten a colored snake path into a single-column strip preserving pixel order.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Non-bg pixels form a single 4-connected path (snake) with exactly two endpoints.\n- Each path pixel may be a different non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two endpoints (cells with only one path neighbor) of the snake.\n- Begin at the top-left-most endpoint and trace the path cell by cell.\n- Output an (N×1) column listing each cell's color in traversal order.\n\nKey insight: The path is a simple chain with no branches; choosing the top-left-most endpoint fixes a unique reading direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8886d717\" lines=\"43-63\">\n<description>\nRepair markers embedded in the main object and copy the remaining markers one pixel toward the solid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly background grid contains one solid non-background edge line.\n- Apart from that edge and the background, there is one large main object\n  color and a smaller marker color.\n- Each marker is either embedded in the main object, touching it on at\n  least three 4-neighbor sides, or isolated on the background.\n\nRule:\n- Embedded markers are recolored to the main object color.\n- Isolated background markers remain in place and also paint the adjacent\n  background pixel in the direction of the solid edge line.\n- All other pixels are unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe full edge line is a direction cue: it tells which way the background\nmarkers extend, while markers surrounded by the main object are simply\ndefects to be filled.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6bcdb01e\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nExtend the edge-touching green start bar through the corridor, turning at each teal blocker until the path leaves the grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- Exactly two foreground colors are present: a 2-pixel 4-connected start bar and many blocker pixels.\n- Exactly one pixel of the start bar lies on the grid border.\n\nRule:\n- Start from the border pixel of the bar and move through the other bar pixel into the grid.\n- Paint every traversed non-blocker pixel with the start color.\n- When the next cell is a blocker, turn to the unique perpendicular non-blocker neighbor; when the next cell is outside the grid, stop.\n\nKey insight: The blocker pixels define a one-cell-wide corridor, so every interior collision has exactly one valid 90-degree turn.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b71a7747\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nExtract the hidden two-color image by compacting the rows and columns of its complete mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid contains one pair of colors whose pixels occupy every cell in the\ncross product of their active rows and active columns; all other colors form\nthe surrounding filler or separators.\nRule\nFind that unique color pair, keep exactly the rows and columns touched by\nthe pair, and return the induced subgrid.\nKey insight\nThe target image is not chosen by color id; it is the only two-color mask\nwhose occupied pixels form a full rectangular row/column product after\ndeleting unused rows and columns.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b5ca7ac4\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nMove bordered 5×5 squares to their designated grid edges by border color.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Azure (8) and red (2) bordered 5×5 squares with colored interiors, scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Azure squares → left edge (col 0); red squares → right edge (col W−5).\n- Each square keeps its original row.\n- Process azure left-to-right, red right-to-left (nearest-to-edge first).\n- If a square's rows overlap an already-placed edge square, place it one square-width inward instead.\n\nKey insight: Near-edge-first ordering plus row-overlap determines which square sits at the edge vs. the second column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3dc255db\" lines=\"37-53\">\n<description>\nEject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\n\nRule:\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\n\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"182e5d0f\" lines=\"89-103\">\n<description>\nMove each gray marker along its green path until it reaches the cell next to a black-green-black gate, erasing the path behind it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground components are made from a path color, a paired gate-end color, and single marker pixels.\n- A gate is a straight end-center-end motif whose center has the path color and whose two ends share the gate-end color.\n\nRule:\n- In every 4-connected foreground component with exactly one marker and one gate, erase the marker and all path-color pixels except the gate center.\n- Place the marker on the unique path-color pixel adjacent to the gate center but not between the two gate ends.\n- Components without both a marker and a gate stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The black-green-black motif marks the stopping gate; the gray marker consumes the connected green path until it rests on the path cell immediately beside that gate.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f83767\" lines=\"23-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the lower color grid by replacing each pixel with its matching top stencil.\n\nInput structure\nThe input has a top legend and a lower dense color grid, separated by blank rows.\nThe legend contains left-to-right 2x2 color swatches and, to their right,\nthe same number of square neutral-color stencil masks.\n\nRule\nPair the swatches and stencil masks by left-to-right order. For every pixel in\nthe lower color grid, paste the paired stencil mask as one output block,\nrecoloring the mask pixels with that pixel's color and leaving mask holes as bg.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower grid is not copied directly; it is an index grid whose colors select\nthe stencil shape from the legend, so each input pixel becomes one square block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"13f06aa5\" lines=\"36-51\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored marker from its directional sprite to a grid edge, painting a dotted ray and the whole edge side.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One repeated body color forms small 8-connected directional sprites.\n- Each marker is a singleton non-background, non-body color embedded in one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- The body pixels around a marker lie mostly behind it; the opposite direction is the marker's ray direction.\n- Paint every second pixel from the marker along that ray, then paint the entire boundary side reached by the ray.\n- If two painted boundary sides meet at a corner, make that shared corner black.\n\nKey insight:\n- The same seven-pixel body sprite rotates to encode the direction; its local center-of-mass bias points away from the ray.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"14b8e18c\" lines=\"40-57\">\n<description>\nMark every complete square object with red pixels just outside its corners.\n\nInput structure:\n- A uniform background contains one or more monochrome 4-connected foreground objects.\n- The relevant objects are square frames or filled squares: their bbox is square\n  and all cells on the bbox border have the object's color.\n- Non-square bars and incomplete fragments may also appear and are ignored.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input unchanged.\n- For each complete square object, paint red on the two outside background\n  pixels orthogonally adjacent to each of the square's four corners.\n\nKey insight: The transformation is not based on size or color; it selects\nobjects whose bounding box is a complete square border and labels only their\nexposed outer corner-adjacent cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d753a70b\" lines=\"81-93\">\n<description>\nRed diamond outlines contract by one layer and gray diamond outlines expand by one layer while other objects stay fixed.\n\nInput structure\n- A 16x16 orange-background grid contains one-cell-thick Manhattan-diamond outlines.\n- Red and gray outlines are the changing objects; other colored diamond outlines are static.\n- Some outlines may be clipped by the grid boundary, but their visible pixels still fit a clipped Manhattan-distance contour.\nRule\n- Clear all red and gray pixels.\n- Red objects are redrawn with the same diamond center and radius minus one.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
